WEBVTT

00:00:00.281 --> 00:00:02.630
Last time, we counted particles in moles.

00:00:03.212 --> 00:00:05.847
But in the lab, nobody counts particles, we

00:00:05.928 --> 00:00:06.310
weigh them.

00:00:07.073 --> 00:00:09.138
This ribbon has a mass of zero point

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.362
two four of a gram.

00:00:10.966 --> 00:00:13.102
So how many moles of magnesium is that?

00:00:13.988 --> 00:00:15.212
To answer it, we need to know the

00:00:15.273 --> 00:00:17.805
mass of one mole of magnesium atoms.

00:00:18.329 --> 00:00:19.801
That's called its molar mass.

00:00:21.257 --> 00:00:23.383
One mole of atoms of any element has

00:00:23.425 --> 00:00:25.832
a mass in grams equal to its relative

00:00:25.954 --> 00:00:26.635
atomic mass.

00:00:27.418 --> 00:00:30.001
Carbon's relative atomic mass is twelve point zero

00:00:30.102 --> 00:00:30.222
one,

00:00:30.783 --> 00:00:32.946
so one mole of carbon atoms has a

00:00:32.987 --> 00:00:34.909
mass of twelve point zero one grams.

00:00:35.672 --> 00:00:38.921
Magnesium's is twenty-four point three one, so one

00:00:38.982 --> 00:00:41.730
mole of magnesium atoms is twenty-four point three

00:00:41.912 --> 00:00:42.392
one grams.

00:00:43.155 --> 00:00:45.319
Each pile holds the same number of atoms,

00:00:45.781 --> 00:00:47.904
six point zero two two times ten to

00:00:47.945 --> 00:00:48.546
the twenty-three.

00:00:49.867 --> 00:00:50.675
Why does that work?

00:00:51.785 --> 00:00:53.940
Relative atomic masses compare atoms.

00:00:54.544 --> 00:00:56.872
A magnesium atom is about twice as heavy

00:00:56.973 --> 00:00:57.795
as a carbon atom.

00:00:58.398 --> 00:01:00.602
So if two piles hold the same number

00:01:00.643 --> 00:01:03.268
of atoms, the magnesium pile must be about

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:04.151
twice as heavy too.

00:01:05.193 --> 00:01:07.695
The mole's number was chosen so the carbon

00:01:07.715 --> 00:01:09.656
pile comes out at about twelve grams,

00:01:10.237 --> 00:01:12.278
and then every other element follows on the

00:01:12.318 --> 00:01:12.919
same scale.

00:01:13.709 --> 00:01:15.798
So the molar mass of a substance is

00:01:15.839 --> 00:01:17.386
the mass of one mole of it.

00:01:17.989 --> 00:01:20.353
Its symbol is M M, and its unit

00:01:20.454 --> 00:01:22.818
is grams per mole, because it tells you

00:01:22.859 --> 00:01:24.602
how many grams each mole weighs.

00:01:25.606 --> 00:01:27.270
For the atoms of an element, take the

00:01:27.332 --> 00:01:29.960
relative atomic mass from the periodic table and

00:01:30.081 --> 00:01:31.184
add grams per mole.

00:01:31.887 --> 00:01:33.428
In this course, use the values on the

00:01:33.489 --> 00:01:36.774
HSC data sheet, like hydrogen, one point zero

00:01:36.795 --> 00:01:39.600
zero eight, and oxygen, sixteen point zero zero.

00:01:40.263 --> 00:01:41.729
Notice the difference from Module one.

00:01:42.312 --> 00:01:45.017
A relative atomic mass has no unit, but

00:01:45.059 --> 00:01:47.423
a molar mass does, grams per mole.

00:01:48.341 --> 00:01:50.124
For a compound, add up the molar masses

00:01:50.284 --> 00:01:51.727
of every atom in the formula,

00:01:52.228 --> 00:01:54.211
just like the relative formula mass from Module

00:01:54.371 --> 00:01:54.472
one.

00:01:55.334 --> 00:01:57.613
Water has two hydrogens and one oxygen.

00:01:58.256 --> 00:02:00.561
Two times one point zero zero eight, plus

00:02:00.601 --> 00:02:03.326
sixteen point zero zero, is eighteen point zero

00:02:03.387 --> 00:02:04.429
two grams per mole.

00:02:05.232 --> 00:02:07.600
Oxygen gas is O two, so its molar

00:02:07.621 --> 00:02:10.512
mass is thirty-two point zero zero, not sixteen.

00:02:11.255 --> 00:02:13.884
And in calcium hydroxide, the two outside the

00:02:13.924 --> 00:02:16.433
bracket doubles both the oxygen and the hydrogen.

00:02:17.509 --> 00:02:19.396
Now we can turn a mass into moles.

00:02:20.079 --> 00:02:21.945
If one mole of water is eighteen point

00:02:21.986 --> 00:02:24.193
zero two grams, how many moles are in

00:02:24.294 --> 00:02:25.237
fifty-four grams?

00:02:25.921 --> 00:02:28.172
Ask how many eighteen point zero twos fit

00:02:28.253 --> 00:02:28.976
into fifty-four.

00:02:29.601 --> 00:02:30.166
That's a division.

00:02:30.751 --> 00:02:33.455
Chemical amount equals mass divided by molar mass,

00:02:34.078 --> 00:02:35.720
n equals m over M M.

00:02:36.444 --> 00:02:37.028
Check the units.

00:02:37.392 --> 00:02:39.829
Grams divided by grams per mole leaves moles.

00:02:40.474 --> 00:02:43.008
And to go back, mass equals moles times

00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:43.571
molar mass.

00:02:44.229 --> 00:02:44.973
Let's finish that one.

00:02:45.619 --> 00:02:47.874
How many moles are in fifty-four grams of

00:02:47.936 --> 00:02:48.237
water?

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:50.867
The molar mass is eighteen point zero two

00:02:50.928 --> 00:02:51.710
grams per mole.

00:02:52.092 --> 00:02:55.073
So n equals fifty-four divided by eighteen point

00:02:55.094 --> 00:02:57.615
zero two, which is three point zero moles

00:02:57.716 --> 00:02:58.596
of water molecules.

00:02:59.317 --> 00:03:01.943
Sense check: fifty-four is three lots of about

00:03:02.085 --> 00:03:03.889
eighteen, so three moles fits.

00:03:04.551 --> 00:03:06.556
And with N A, that's about one point

00:03:06.677 --> 00:03:08.883
eight times ten to the twenty-four molecules.

00:03:09.801 --> 00:03:10.369
Quick prediction.

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:13.090
Ten grams of magnesium, or ten grams of

00:03:13.151 --> 00:03:13.532
copper.

00:03:13.896 --> 00:03:15.105
Which contains more atoms?

00:03:19.137 --> 00:03:19.700
Magnesium.

00:03:20.244 --> 00:03:21.645
A copper atom is about two and a

00:03:21.706 --> 00:03:23.968
half times as heavy as a magnesium atom,

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:26.331
with a molar mass of sixty-three point five

00:03:26.392 --> 00:03:29.135
five, so each gram of copper holds fewer

00:03:29.236 --> 00:03:29.515
atoms.

00:03:30.098 --> 00:03:32.269
Ten grams of magnesium is about zero point

00:03:32.331 --> 00:03:33.235
four one moles.

00:03:33.578 --> 00:03:35.563
Ten grams of copper is only about zero

00:03:35.604 --> 00:03:36.686
point one six moles.

00:03:37.248 --> 00:03:39.457
The same mass doesn't mean the same amount.

00:03:40.062 --> 00:03:42.075
Lighter particles mean more of them in each

00:03:42.116 --> 00:03:42.397
gram.

00:03:42.999 --> 00:03:43.568
Now the other way.

00:03:44.095 --> 00:03:47.021
What mass of sodium chloride contains zero point

00:03:47.103 --> 00:03:48.124
five zero moles?

00:03:48.867 --> 00:03:51.616
Sodium chloride is ionic, so we count formula

00:03:51.717 --> 00:03:54.687
units, each one sodium ion and one chloride

00:03:54.768 --> 00:03:54.948
ion.

00:03:55.531 --> 00:03:57.797
The molar mass is twenty-two point nine nine

00:03:58.139 --> 00:04:01.368
plus thirty-five point four five, fifty-eight point four

00:04:01.449 --> 00:04:02.512
four grams per mole.

00:04:03.195 --> 00:04:05.823
Mass equals moles times molar mass.

00:04:06.506 --> 00:04:09.137
Zero point five zero times fifty-eight point four

00:04:09.178 --> 00:04:11.247
four is about twenty-nine grams.

00:04:12.152 --> 00:04:14.121
That's three point zero times ten to the

00:04:14.162 --> 00:04:15.567
twenty-three formula units.

00:04:16.644 --> 00:04:17.770
Now the ribbon from the start.

00:04:18.272 --> 00:04:20.295
Zero point two four of a gram, divided

00:04:20.336 --> 00:04:22.679
by twenty-four point three one grams per mole,

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:25.323
is about zero point zero zero nine nine

00:04:25.424 --> 00:04:26.926
moles of magnesium atoms.

00:04:27.608 --> 00:04:30.014
Times N A, that's about five point nine

00:04:30.095 --> 00:04:31.820
times ten to the twenty-one atoms.

00:04:32.987 --> 00:04:34.861
Mass to moles uses molar mass.

00:04:35.407 --> 00:04:36.999
Moles to particles uses N A.

00:04:37.906 --> 00:04:40.011
Moles sit in the middle, the bridge between

00:04:40.072 --> 00:04:41.836
what you can weigh and what you can't

00:04:41.897 --> 00:04:42.177
count.

00:04:42.983 --> 00:04:43.848
Here's a line to remember.

00:04:44.433 --> 00:04:45.747
Let the units decide.

00:04:46.332 --> 00:04:49.591
Grams divided by grams per mole leaves moles.

00:04:50.033 --> 00:04:51.797
So if a mass is given in kilograms,

00:04:52.098 --> 00:04:54.183
change it to grams first, or the units

00:04:54.203 --> 00:04:54.704
won't cancel.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:04:57.713
Your turn.

00:04:58.339 --> 00:05:01.111
Calculate the chemical amount in eleven point zero

00:05:01.153 --> 00:05:02.739
grams of carbon dioxide.

00:05:03.222 --> 00:05:04.024
Pause here to answer.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.202
The molar mass of carbon dioxide is twelve

00:05:17.262 --> 00:05:19.864
point zero one plus two times sixteen point

00:05:19.904 --> 00:05:22.707
zero zero, forty-four point zero one grams per

00:05:22.727 --> 00:05:22.927
mole.

00:05:23.588 --> 00:05:26.535
So n equals eleven point zero divided by

00:05:26.615 --> 00:05:27.777
forty-four point zero one,

00:05:28.579 --> 00:05:30.162
zero point two five zero moles.

00:05:31.205 --> 00:05:34.112
Multiplying instead gives four hundred and eighty-four, which

00:05:34.332 --> 00:05:36.557
can't be right, because eleven grams is far

00:05:36.617 --> 00:05:37.379
less than one mole.

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:39.533
Last one.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:43.291
Calculate the mass of zero point one five

00:05:43.372 --> 00:05:45.921
zero moles of calcium hydroxide.

00:05:46.488 --> 00:05:47.300
Pause here to answer.

00:05:58.075 --> 00:06:00.700
The bracket doubles the oxygen and the hydrogen,

00:06:01.142 --> 00:06:03.546
so the molar mass is seventy-four point one

00:06:03.627 --> 00:06:04.829
zero grams per mole.

00:06:05.691 --> 00:06:08.556
Mass equals moles times molar mass, zero point

00:06:08.797 --> 00:06:11.681
one five zero times seventy-four point one zero,

00:06:12.023 --> 00:06:13.745
which is eleven point one grams.

00:06:14.627 --> 00:06:17.069
Ignoring the bracket gives eight point five six

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:18.690
grams, the slip to avoid.

00:06:20.736 --> 00:06:22.785
Every molar mass today came from a formula.

00:06:23.309 --> 00:06:24.818
But what if you have a substance and

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:25.946
don't know its formula?

00:06:26.613 --> 00:06:27.546
Turn the equation around.

00:06:28.074 --> 00:06:30.633
Molar mass equals mass divided by moles.

00:06:31.464 --> 00:06:32.396
Weighing gives the mass.

00:06:32.839 --> 00:06:34.344
If we can find the moles some other

00:06:34.365 --> 00:06:36.071
way, we can measure the molar mass.

00:06:36.938 --> 00:06:38.672
Next time, we'll see how a practical does

00:06:38.713 --> 00:06:38.773
it.

00:06:41.461 --> 00:06:42.284
Here are the key notes.

00:06:42.807 --> 00:06:44.355
Pause the video if you need more time

00:06:44.395 --> 00:06:44.817
to copy them.
